  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This commentary has begun to attract focused scholarly attention, with 2 citations and 4 documented downstream reuses. The citation trail is still early, but it already reaches a small set of plant-science venues and adjacent scholarly formats, suggesting that the piece is being read as a conceptual contribution to ongoing debates about carnivorous plant evolution and jasmonate signaling.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Procko and Chory\u2019s article is a short, theory-oriented commentary in the <em>Journal of Experimental Botany</em> that engages with questions about whether jasmonate signaling has been broadly co-opted across carnivorous plants. Its impact profile fits that role: the work has a modest but visible citation record, and the citing literature points to continued use in discussions of carnivorous plant biology rather than broad public attention.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest downstream signal is scholarly reuse. Beyond the two citations captured in OpenAlex, the record shows four documented reuses, including an article, a book chapter, and two biomedical article mentions indexed by Europe PMC. The citing landscape is especially concentrated in plant-focused scholarship and related educational settings, with reuse appearing in venues such as <em>New Phytologist</em> and a book chapter on carnivorous plants as model species. That pattern suggests the commentary is functioning as a reference point for specialist synthesis and review.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The attention record is otherwise quiet. No event-data mentions are available, and the scite record contains only a single mentioning entry, so the evidence points to limited but real uptake rather than broad cross-platform discussion. The article is open-access in hybrid form, and repository records show multiple accessible instances, which may help explain its circulation within the research community.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Funding and authorship signals place the work within the Salk Institute for Biological Studies, supported by NIH-related funding and the Howard Hughes Medical Institute. Overall, the record shows an early-stage citation profile with clear specialist reuse: this is a conceptual plant biology commentary that is being taken up in the literature, even though the broader public or media footprint is not yet visible in the available data.</p>",
